<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.chime-player — ONE resident coreaudiod client for every chime hooks/notify.sh plays
  (2026-10-10). scripts/chime-player.sh pins the spool dir and execs scripts/chime-player.js under
  `osascript -l JavaScript`, which polls the spool every 0.25 s and plays each request with a cached
  AppKit NSSound.

  🚨 STAGED, NOT LOADED. It lives in launchd/staged/, outside install.sh's launchd/*.plist glob, on
  purpose: that glob copies every plist into ~/Library/LaunchAgents, and launchd loads everything
  there at the next login, so a copy IS an activation (tests/install-staged-plist.bats). Loading it
  changes how every chime is played, which is the operator's call (C10):
  docs/activation/pending-activation/51-chime-player-activate.sh installs, loads and verifies it.

  Every chime used to be a fresh afplay, i.e. a NEW coreaudiod client, ~300 a day. Measured
  2026-10-10 from coreaudiod's own log: a new client process costs 3 TCCAccessRequest calls in
  coreaudiod; one long-lived process playing three sounds paid 3 at its first play and 0 for the
  next two. On 2026-10-09 one chime's permission check parked coreaudiod ~7 minutes behind a stalled
  trustd/secd and froze Hammerspoon. afplay clients also leak coreaudiod IO contexts under load
  (docs/research/coreaudiod-spin-2026-09-29.md).

  KeepAlive + RunAtLoad, no StartInterval: the cadence lives in the script, as in
  com.claude.compressor-sentinel. launchd's whole job is to start it and restart it if it dies.

  ThrottleInterval 30, not launchd's default 10: each (re)start is a new audio client and pays the
  permission check again, so a crash loop at the default floor would be the per-chime client storm
  this job exists to end, merely renamed. 30 s bounds that at 2 starts a minute. While the player is
  down its heartbeat goes stale after 15 s and notify.sh DROPS chimes rather than falling back to
  afplay, so a crash loop costs chimes, never a client storm.

  No ProcessType, so the Standard band: it plays user-facing audio, and a Background-band job is
  deferred under exactly the load at which chimes are being raised (compressor-sentinel's header has
  the measurement). Nice and LowPriorityIO are omitted for the same reason.

  Kill switches: CC_NOTIFY_CHIME_PLAYER=off in the hook's environment (notify.sh goes back to afplay),
  or unload it AND delete its heartbeat, both, in one line:
    launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.claude.chime-player; rm -f "$(getconf DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR)cc-chime/heartbeat"
  A bootout alone is NOT a kill switch: the heartbeat file outlives the process, notify.sh reads it
  as a stale (wedged) player, and DROPS every chime until the next reboot clears the temp dir. Only
  an absent heartbeat means "no player, use afplay". The uninstall line 51 prints does both and also
  removes the LaunchAgents copy, so the next login does not load it again.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.chime-player</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; exec /bin/bash "$HOME/.claude/scripts/chime-player.sh"</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>30</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/chime-player.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/chime-player.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
